.TH AWK 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
awk \- pattern-directed scanning and processing language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B awk
.RB [ \-F
.IR fs " |"
.BR \-\-csv ]
.RB [ \-v
.IR var = value ]
.RB [ \-safe ]
.I 'prog'
.RI [ file " ...]"
.br
.B awk
.RB [ \-F
.IR fs " |"
.BR \-\-csv ]
.RB [ \-v
.IR var = value ]
.RB [ \-safe ]
.B \-f
.I progfile
\&...
.RI [ file " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
Substrate's
.B awk
is Brian Kernighan's \(lqOne True Awk\(rq, compiled unmodified from
the vendored sources in
.B contrib/onetrueawk
(upstream version 20260426).
.PP
.B awk
scans each input
.I file
for lines that match any of a set of patterns given literally in
.I prog
or in one or more files named with
.BR \-f .
Each pattern may have an associated action that is performed when a
line matches.
The file name
.B \-
means standard input; with no
.I file
operands, standard input is read.
An operand of the form
.IB var = value
is treated as an assignment, performed at the time the operand would
have been opened as a file.
.PP
An input record is normally a line, split into fields by white space
or by the regular expression
.BR FS .
Fields are
.BR $1 ,
.BR $2 ,
\&...;
.B $0
is the whole record.
If
.B FS
is empty, each character is a separate field.
.SS Program structure
A program is a sequence of
.IP
.IB pattern " { " action " }"
.PP
statements, separated by newlines or semicolons.
A missing action prints the record; a missing pattern always matches.
Functions may be defined in place of a pattern-action statement:
.IP
.B function
.IB name ( "parameter-list" )
.B {
.I statements
.B }
.PP
Scalar arguments are passed by value and arrays by reference;
parameters are local and all other variables are global, so extra
parameters serve as local variables.
.B func
is accepted as a synonym for
.BR function .
.SS Statements
.EX
if (\fIexpr\fP) \fIstatement\fP [else \fIstatement\fP]
while (\fIexpr\fP) \fIstatement\fP
for (\fIexpr\fP; \fIexpr\fP; \fIexpr\fP) \fIstatement\fP
for (\fIvar\fP in \fIarray\fP) \fIstatement\fP
do \fIstatement\fP while (\fIexpr\fP)
break
continue
{ [\fIstatement\fP ...] }
\fIexpr\fP
print [\fIexpr-list\fP] [> \fIexpr\fP | >> \fIexpr\fP | | \fIexpr\fP]
printf \fIformat\fP [, \fIexpr-list\fP] [> \fIexpr\fP | >> \fIexpr\fP | | \fIexpr\fP]
return [\fIexpr\fP]
next
nextfile
delete \fIarray\fP[\fIexpr\fP]
delete \fIarray\fP
exit [\fIexpr\fP]
.EE
.PP
.B next
skips the remaining patterns for the current record;
.B nextfile
skips the rest of the current input file.
An empty expression list stands for
.BR $0 .
Output files and pipes named by identical strings in different
statements refer to the same open stream.
.SS Expressions
String constants are written in double quotes with the usual C
escapes.
Values are strings or numbers as context requires.
The operators are
.BR "+ \- * / % ^" ,
concatenation (juxtaposition),
.BR "! ++ \-\- += \-= *= /= %= ^= > >= < <= == != ?:" ,
.BR "&& ||" ,
the match operators
.B ~
and
.BR !~ ,
and
.BR in .
Variables are scalars, fields, or associative arrays indexed by
strings; a subscript
.B [i,j]
is joined with
.BR SUBSEP .
Uninitialised variables have the null string (and numeric zero) as
value.
.SS Patterns
A pattern is an arbitrary Boolean combination of regular expressions
(written
.BI / re /\fR,\fP
using
.BR egrep (1)
syntax) and relational expressions.
A bare regular expression matches against
.BR $0 .
Two patterns separated by a comma select the range of records from a
match of the first through the next match of the second, inclusive.
The special patterns
.B BEGIN
and
.B END
match before the first record is read and after the last; they may
appear more than once and do not combine with other patterns.
.SS Built-in functions
.TP
.BR atan2 ( \fIy\fP , \fIx\fP "), " cos "(\fIx\fP), " exp "(\fIx\fP), " \
int "(\fIx\fP), " log "(\fIx\fP), " sin "(\fIx\fP), " sqrt (\fIx\fP)
Arithmetic functions;
.B int
truncates toward zero.
.TP
.BR rand ()
Random number in [0,1).
.TP
.BR srand ([ \fIs\fP ])
Seed
.B rand
with
.I s
(or the time of day) and return the previous seed.
The initial seed is 1.
.TP
.BR length ([ \fIv\fP ])
Length of
.I v
as a string, number of elements if
.I v
is an array, or length of
.B $0
with no argument.
.TP
.BR substr ( \fIs\fP , " \fIm\fP" [, " \fIn\fP" ])
The
.IR n -character
substring of
.I s
starting at position
.I m
(counting from 1), or the rest of the string without
.IR n .
.TP
.BR index ( \fIs\fP , " \fIt\fP" )
Position of
.I t
in
.IR s ,
or 0.
.TP
.BR match ( \fIs\fP , " \fIr\fP" )
Position in
.I s
where the regular expression
.I r
matches, or 0; sets
.B RSTART
and
.BR RLENGTH .
.TP
.BR split ( \fIs\fP , " \fIa\fP" [, " \fIfs\fP" ])
Split
.I s
into
.IB a [1]\fR,\fP
\&...,
.IB a [ n ]
using
.I fs
(default
.BR FS )
and return
.IR n .
An empty
.I fs
splits into characters.
.TP
.BR sub ( \fIr\fP , " \fIt\fP" [, " \fIs\fP" ])
Replace the first match of
.I r
in
.I s
(default
.BR $0 )
by
.IR t ;
.B &
in
.I t
stands for the matched text.
Returns the number of replacements.
.TP
.BR gsub ( \fIr\fP , " \fIt\fP" [, " \fIs\fP" ])
As
.BR sub ,
but replace every match.
.TP
.BR sprintf ( \fIfmt\fP , " \fIexpr\fP" ", ...)"
Format the expressions as
.BR printf (3)
would.
.TP
.BR system ( \fIcmd\fP )
Run
.I cmd
with the shell and return its exit status: \-1 on error, the exit
status on normal exit, 256 plus the signal number if killed by a
signal, or 512 plus the signal number if a core was dumped.
.TP
.BR tolower ( \fIs\fP "), " toupper ( \fIs\fP )
Case-converted copy of
.IR s .
.TP
.BR close ( \fIexpr\fP )
Close the file or pipe named
.IR expr .
.TP
.BR fflush ([ \fIexpr\fP ])
Flush output buffered for
.IR expr ,
or for all output streams.
.PP
.B getline
reads the next record into
.B $0
from the current input;
.BI "getline <" " file"
reads from
.IR file ;
.BI "getline " var
sets
.I var
instead of
.BR $0 ;
and
.IB cmd " | getline"
reads successive lines of the output of
.IR cmd .
.B getline
returns 1 on success, 0 at end of file, and \-1 on error.
.SS Special variables
.TP
.BR ARGC ", " ARGV
Argument count and array; both are assignable, and non-null members
of
.B ARGV
are taken as input file names.
.TP
.B CONVFMT
Number-to-string conversion format (default
.BR %.6g ).
.TP
.B ENVIRON
The environment, indexed by variable name.
Empty in
.B \-safe
mode.
.TP
.B FILENAME
Name of the current input file.
.TP
.B FNR
Record number in the current file.
.TP
.B FS
Input field separator (default a blank).
.TP
.B NF
Number of fields in the current record.
.TP
.B NR
Record number over all input.
.TP
.B OFMT
Output format for numbers (default
.BR %.6g ).
.TP
.B OFS
Output field separator (default a blank).
.TP
.B ORS
Output record separator (default newline).
.TP
.BR RLENGTH ", " RSTART
Length and start of the text matched by
.BR match .
.TP
.B RS
Input record separator (default newline).
If empty, records are separated by blank lines; if longer than one
character, it is treated as a regular expression.
.TP
.B SUBSEP
Subscript separator (default
.BR \e034 ).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-F " fs"
Set the input field separator to the regular expression
.IR fs .
.B \-Ft
sets it to a tab.
.TP
.B \-\-csv
Parse input as comma-separated values.
Setting
.B FS
at the same time draws a warning.
.TP
.BI \-v " var" = value
Assign
.I value
to
.I var
before the program starts, including before
.BR BEGIN .
May be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-f " progfile"
Read the program from
.IR progfile ;
.B \-
means standard input.
May be repeated; the files are concatenated.
.TP
.B \-safe
Disallow the
.B system
function (rejected as a syntax error) and leave
.B ENVIRON
empty.
.TP
.BI \-d [ n ]
Enable parser/interpreter debugging output at level
.I n
(default 1).
.TP
.BR \-version ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of options.
.PP
Unknown options produce a warning and are ignored.
Option arguments may be attached
.RB ( \-F: )
or separate
.RB ( "\-F :" ).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal completion.
.TP
.I n
The value given to an
.B exit
statement.
.TP
.B 2
A syntax error or fatal runtime error.
.TP
.B 1
No arguments at all were given (usage message).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set,
.B sub
and
.B gsub
follow the POSIX rules for runs of backslashes before
.B &
in the replacement string.
.PP
The whole environment is available through
.B ENVIRON
unless
.B \-safe
is given.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print lines longer than 72 characters:
.PP
.EX
awk \(aqlength($0) > 72\(aq
.EE
.PP
Swap the first two fields:
.PP
.EX
awk \(aq{ print $2, $1 }\(aq
.EE
.PP
Sum the first column and print the total and average:
.PP
.EX
awk \(aq{ s += $1 } END { print "sum is", s, "average is", s/NR }\(aq
.EE
.PP
Print the login names from the password file:
.PP
.EX
awk \-F: \(aq{ print $1 }\(aq /etc/passwd
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR egrep (1),
.BR grep (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR tr (1),
.BR printf (3),
.BR regex (3)
.PP
A. V. Aho, B. W. Kernighan, P. J. Weinberger,
.IR "The AWK Programming Language, Second Edition" ,
Addison-Wesley, 2024.
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2024
.BR awk ,
with the extensions
.BR \-\-csv ,
.BR \-safe ,
.BR nextfile ,
.BR fflush ,
.BR delete " of whole arrays, and"
.BR ENVIRON .
.SH BUGS
There are no explicit conversions between numbers and strings; add 0
to force a number, or concatenate
.B \(dq\(dq
to force a string.
.PP
Input is expected to be UTF-8; other multibyte encodings are not
handled.
.PP
Strings are converted to numbers after pre-filtering: hexadecimal
strings convert to 0, and only
.BR +nan ,
.BR \-nan ,
.BR +inf ,
and
.B \-inf
(in any case) convert to NaN or infinity.
